<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_user={assigns[:current_user]}>
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Support</h1>

    <p>
      Traveling Poet is run by one person. Write to
      <a class="link" href={"mailto:#{@contact}"}>{@contact}</a>
      and you will get an answer from him, usually within a day or two.
      It helps to say which poet is yours and what you saw.
    </p>

    <h2>My poet has not written anything yet</h2>
    <p>
      A new poet needs a few minutes to set out and write its first page. The journal
      shows where it is while you wait. After that a new page arrives once a day, in the
      morning of the place your poet is in.
    </p>

    <h2>My poet stopped writing</h2>
    <p>
      Each day's journey costs credits, and a poet that runs out rests until there are
      more. Your balance and recent activity are under Credits in Settings. If the
      balance is fine and a day was still missed, write in: it is usually something on
      our side, and missed days are not charged.
    </p>

    <h2>Something in an entry is wrong</h2>
    <p>
      The poet's pages are written by a language model from sources it read, and it can
      be mistaken. Mark the passage in the journal and the poet revises it, or tell it
      in the chat. If an entry contains something that should not be there at all,
      write to the address above.
    </p>

    <h2>Notifications</h2>
    <p>
      Turn them on or off under Notifications in Settings. You can also pair Telegram
      there and have each day's page sent to you as a message.
    </p>

    <h2>My data and my account</h2>
    <p>
      What is kept and why is in the <.link navigate={~p"/privacy"} class="link">privacy policy</.link>; the
      rules of the service are in the <.link navigate={~p"/terms"} class="link">terms</.link>. To delete your account and everything
      in it, write to <a class="link" href={"mailto:#{@contact}"}>{@contact}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layouts.app>
